My second ascension this month, and my second of all time :) It seems that
once Nethack
*clicks*, you're in good shape from then on... it's a lot more fun to play
now, that's for
sure. My previous ascension was Chumpy the Valk. I nearly had a second
earlier with a Wizard,
but some foolish choices and a lack of preparation for the Wizard of Yendor
doomed that
character to an ignoble death. I applied what I learned there to this
character, and equipped
myself heartily with dual wielded +7 weapons, wands of death, and backup
scrolls of charging in
case.

I was playing random male characters, wound up with a Chaotic Orcish
Barbarian. The application
of an amulet of change (in potential preparation for attempting egg
laying...) resulted in
ascension to Demigoddess-hood. Perhaps he'll hang out with Chumpy (the
unfortunately named
Valk, who actually *was* female from creation :) up at the dieties bar.

Early game went fairly smoothly, I zipped down to Sokoban, cleared up to
near the top. Took the
food and backtracked briefly to the mines, descending to Minetown. Spent
some time sacrificing
there and at an altar in the main dungeons, and acquired Cleaver. A nice big
two handed axe,
quite appropriate I thought. Though still leery of two handed weapons after
my horrid
experience with a quarerstaff, I enchanted it when possible and wielded it
for a good long
time. I returned to Sokoban, finished clearing it (Amulet of Reflection,
which I wore for a
very long time), then returned to Mines and went to the bottom to get the
Luckstone.

Once this early work was done, I began descending deeper, looking for the
Quest and Ludios. I
did not find Ludios this game (bummer!), and partly as a result, did not
acquire full
protection (-7 or so?). I was too young to attempt the quest when I arrived,
so I descended all
the way to the Castle first. I was hoping Medusa's level would have a Shield
of Reflection to
replace the amulet - but that didn't happen, and I still hadn't acquired a
replacement weapon
for Cleaver, despite mass sacrificing. The rest of my armor was also rather
poor at this point,
so I figured procuring the wand of wishing in the castle would aid my kit
significantly.

I picked up the passtune for the first time this game, and used that to both
open the gate, and
smush various unfriendlies who stepped foot on it (muhaha!). A ring of
Conflict (plus checking
the area first with telepathy) guaranteed that clearing out the interior of
the castle was
fairly safe. Cleaver made short work of the Dragons guarding the loot, but
unfortunately, no
useful scales, and not a single useful item in the loot horde.

Undeterred, I went and grabbed the wand, then considered my kit. First
wish - 2 blessed scrolls
of charging. Second wish - You wrench one last charge from the wand... doh!
Considerably
bummed, I mulled this over for a few moments. My armor wasn't great, but I
figured I could
acquire more armor relatively easily. My god had yet to gift me with a
useful one handed weapon
(I was still thinking of perhaps finding Ludios and getting a Shield of
Reflection, maybe with
polypiling), so I decided to attempt wishing for an artifact weapon. Frost
Brand then! For a
moment you feel something in your hands... doh! That'll teach me to wish for
artifacts!
Probably a host of other factors I'm unaware of that could get me in trouble
as well.

Ok, so that didn't turn out so well. Determined to get something useful out
of my diety, I went
back up touting several wands of create monster and dutifully sacrificed
away. First gift?
Frost Brand... ;) Ah well! I had picked up a Silver Saber somewhere earlier
and practiced with
it, so a bit of practice with Frost Brand and I decided to give two-weapon
combat a try. Still
no shield of reflection, so why not? I was bummed to discover that you can't
wield two
artifacts at once (my diety had given me one of the more useless slayers -
trollbane iirc), but
Frost Brand + Silver Saber seemed a potent combination in any event.

It turned out to be just that, as I went to my Quest, descended to Thoth
Amon's lair, smashed
him in the face with a Potion of Sleep, and then cut him apart. The Heart of
Ahriman is mine!
What does this thing do? Hmm, peruse spoilers. Stealth... which my diety had
already granted.
Levitation... which I already had a ring for. And a luckstone. Eh. Not as
cool as the Valk's or
Wizards for sure, but hey, no problem. Down we go!

Only issue being now... my armor is good, but not great. Mildly enchanted,
T-Shirt, Grey Dragon
Scale Mail, Oilskin Cloak, Dwarven Helm, Jumping Boots, no gloves (this
bloody near got me
killed later).

I contemplated the situation for awhile, but then decided to descend into
the Valley and
Gehennom (Heck^2 patch installed). This worked out well. My defense wasn't
spectacular, but I
was cautious, and the offensive power of dual wielding was extremely potent.
Even Dragons would
go down in just a few rounds, and all of the demons I encountered dropped
rapidly. I had
expected dual wielding would have a significant accuracy penalty, but it
seemed that the Silver
Saber in my offhand was hitting every time.

Descending into Gehennom resulted in some new Heck^2 levels I had not seen
previously - very
large open spaces, which made me nervous, and some strangely laid out levels
with some
undiggable walls. This made me nervous, after my last experience with the
Wizard near the
bottom between the Vibrating Square level and a special Heck level above it
caused me serious
grief. By this point, I had genocided h, L, ;. I left out c this game, and
that almost got me
killed. Remember I said no gloves? Well, I found a cockatrice, *almost*
picked it up, but since
my danger alarms were at 100% around c's of all sorts, I carefully examined
my equipment before
doing so, and noticed to my horror that I was wearing no gloves. Yeeks.

I dropped down as far as Orcus' level, and here I hit a break. Resting
peacefully in an empty
room in the top right of the level was a fully charged wand of wishing! In
one of the rooms, I
also found a magic lamp :) The convenient altar in the center of the level
provided a great
resting point to identify the status of gear and mull over my kit. I
ultimately wound up
wishing up a different set of gear, to swap out the Amulet of Reflection
that I'd been using so
long, in case I wanted to wear either an Amulet of Lifesaving, or the
Magical Breathing amulet
(which proved mighty handy). I also wanted ESP of some sort, since I was
going to swap out one
of my rings.

I wished up Speed Boots, Silver Dragon Scale Mail, a Cloak of Magic
Resistantance,
a Helm of Telepathy (nice item! as in my other games, I almost never seem to
find these
normally), a Ring of Free Action, a magic marker, and saved the last wish
for later (which I
wound up using on another magic marker, to magic map Gehennom for my
sanity). I also found
Gauntlets of Dexterity here (at -3 :P), and figured why not, so I enchanted
and used those. Is
there a signifcant accuracy or ac bonus for having high dex? I never seemed
to miss while dual
wielding anyway... I considered wishing up Gauntlets of Power, but my str
seemed adaquete for
the weight I was carrying. Another factor here was that I realized my gear
would be (with some
slightly different choices) casting friendly. I aborted this option however,
as my PP was
absolutely abyssmal, despite some efforts to increase it using potions and
magic traps (for the
first time ever, neat trick). Plus without skill in anything but Attack
spells, I couldn't use
the spells that save my sanity (Magic Mapping particularly, though Id is
handy, I still had un
id'd rings by games end).

Once this was done, I began to feel a bit more confident, enchanted my gear
as best as I was
able, and went on to whack Orcus. I then proceeded with my preparations for
waking the Wizard.
I had already dug out routes between the stairs in the main dungeon, but I
also dug out routes
in Gehennom, found the VS, dropped the Candalabrum and Bell there, and used
my last wish for
another magic marker to get some Magic Mapping scrolls. I was pleased that
the MM was able to
generate plenty of mapping, so I was able to get the rest of Gehennom mapped
and dug out in a
much shorter timespan than it took for the descent to Orcus (which, while
mitigated somewhat by
Heck's special levels, was still generally painful)

One commentary on Gehennom - while Magic Mapping is a huge boon, *LIGHT* is
another serious
issue. Infravision (for being Orcish) was certainly handy, but for locating
items, cold
monsters, and stairs, a lamp is near essential. I found that dealing with
regular lamps was
almost impossible (though I would bring them along and use them), the micro
of turning them on
and off while digging etc was a pain. This time, I skirted the issue by
using my magic lamp as,
well, a lamp :) This plus the MM scrolls made Gehennom *much* more bearable
this game.

When all was prepared, I did a final level teleport up to the top, grabbed
candles at Izchak's
(I'm not _that_ confident yet :P), cleared out the routes, drank all
fountains for a wish
(didn't get one), double checked my inventory, prepared a second sack with
remove curse/holy
water/backup uni horn, ditched my pickaxe finally, and entered the Wizards
tower for the second
time (I'd already gone in once to clear a path through the critters while I
was down there).
I woke up the Wiz, blasted him with the Wand of Death, descended rapidly to
the Vibrating
Square, performed the invocation, and entered the Sanctum. Stealth is great!
The entire first
room was asleep, and I carefully searched my way there to avoid squeaky
boards. I whacked the
room easily, then made my way to the second. This room was awake, and the
Priests of Moloch
were ornery, but again the dual wielding cut them down with a quickness.
Once the route was
clear, I located the door to the chamber, went in, zapped the head Priest
with a wand of death,
grabbed the Amulet, and named it.

Now I was ready to get out! I returned to the upstairs, and used up my piles
of teleportation
scrolls I'd been saving, intrinsic teleportation, wands of teleport, and
zipped between the
down and upstairs as fast as I could. Perhaps due to this speed, The Wizard
only showed up a
few times, and I quickly blasted him into oblivion with the wands.

A few hundred turns later, I was sitting at Dlvl 1, excited, and ready to
tackle the Planes :)
This time I'd brought along a bundle of prepatory gear, now that I had a
better idea of what to
expect. Expired spell? Check. Gold detection? Check. Earth - Wiz showed up,
zap! Plenty of
wands of digging brought along, made my way to portal. Air was silly - I
forgot to put on my
ring of levitation immediately and spent several turns spinning in place
feebly trying to fight
the monsters around me until I figured out what was going on. Put on the
ring inside a steam
vortex (YAFM I've never seen :D), hopped out, confused and read scroll,
zipped over to portal.
Fire - Kept levitation on, detected portal, ran straight over. Water I was
hoping would be over
quickly. No such luck :P Detected the portal, and it vanished on the next
turn. Drat!
Considering this for a few moments, I remembered that I could use Levitation
here as well (so,
so strange), and made my way over to where I had spotted it after detecting.
This apparently
worked, as I shortly hit the portal to the Astral Plane :)

I arrived at the Astral Plane without Conflict on (wanted to try it straight
this time). Got a
peaceful Angel companion, but as I suspected, he didn't prove to be
particularly useful (which
is to say, at all). I tried the center temple first. Pestilence was the
guardian, making me
very nervous. He got me sick once, but I backed off, healed, and kept him at
a distance,
zapping him with WoD (even recharging it using my backup MM when needed). He
went down, checked
temple. Crom! Crom and Steel! Curses. Ok. Headed over to the right temple
this time. Famine...
Hmm. Zap zap zap. Dead. Cool :) Entered temple, strolled over to check and
yes indeed, a black
altar of Set. Offer the amulet and Kain the confused demigoddess ascends to
Glory :)

General comments on gameplay follow:

I made much heavier use of wands this game, keeping stuff at a distance. I
also made some
effort to use and enchant daggers, but I didn't get enough excess scrolls to
do this, and it
did not prove necessary (the wands + dual wielding dealt with all normal
foes quite swiftly).

I also did the 'name gems in piles and toss them at unicorns' trick. I don't
know how to gauge when my luck maxes out, but it did seem handy to more
quickly identify all the gems I found in the dungeon, though I did not make
a concentrated effort to collect them as I did with my Wizard (losing him
with a giant stash of gems made me leery of wasting that much rl time just
to lose it due to a stupid death at some point along the line).

Did a little nurse dancing, guzzled all the healing potions I could find. I
was probably using Nurses very unsafely (down in Gehennom), but Warning is
so nice (as is ESP), that I felt fairly safe. The HP boost was definitely
worth it. Golden Glows + Nurses + Potions pushed me up from ~120ish hp to my
endgame ~290.

No luck with getting my int/cha up high enough to use foocubi. I
contemplated a helm of brilliance for this, but I judged I had enough hp/ac
to deal with the combat I was likely to encounter, and curiously, raising
your level as a melee counterintuitively makes the game *more* difficult,
not less, unless I'm missing something (due to monster difficulty).
Encountered Salamanders for the first time ever this game...

Descending into Gehennom with a much weaker set of gear than I'm used to was
somewhat nervous,
but I was careful, and it worked out very well (thanks Orcus!). Instadeath
on the Wiz of Yendor
and the two Riders I encountered was also much less stressful. I did not
know you could ^T to
teleport prior to this game! Had been reading the manual over and found the
key. Couldn't
figure out what it was for until I realized you had to have Teleportitis
(not just a ring of
teleportation apparently). Unfortunately it took PP to use (of which I did
not have many), but
its a useful piece of info I'll file away. Could be very handy for the next
caster I attempt.

I did not acquire many resistances until fairly late in this game (bad luck
with corpses I guess). Somewhat related to this, I also remembered someone
mentioning Magical Breathing preventing choking, and used that to guzzle
down a ton of Gelee Royale, plus giant corpses. Funny messages :) Gave me a
good strength to lug stuff around.

I made certain to actually just flat out *pray* this game, each time I
reached mollified/reconcilled, and I did ultimately get crowed by my diety.
I also got a bunch of Golden Glows, Stealth (neat!), a few points of
Protection (still didn't max it out due to lack of gold till near the end of
the game, and not sure it was fully max then). Even a few handy spellbooks
(though I couldn't make good use of them). Stormbringer, the crowning gift,
didn't seem particularly useful. Perusing the comments mentioned demons
being immune to life drain. So while that sounded quite cool, I didn't want
to whack peaceful monsters with it (incidently, what is the penalty for
attacking a peaceful, non pet monster? I had to do so a few times, but
didn't seem to suffer loss of protection or telepathy - do I lose luck or
anger my god?).

Dual wielding is amazingly powerful, I'm not sure if I'll bother with a
Shield of Reflection
for the classes that are capable of utilizing two weapons in the future. I
wish ranged combat
was easier in Nethack in general :( The weight + fussiness of dealing with
ranged weapons means
that just a stack of daggers and some wands is preferable to most other
types of ranged combat
(bow, xbow, darts, shuriken).

Does anyone know how to suppress the 'You begin to hack at the wall with
your Pickaxe' message?
Would help make digging out Gehennom + stair routes that much faster.

Hopefully formatting is ok for this post, I printscreen-pasted each screen
of my ascension (windows dos-console version) and pasted them into a text
file. That follows.

Many thanks to the group regulars for advice before and after my first AP
earlier this month,
and again on my Wizard YASD - it proved to be very useful! Particularly in
terms of making
Gehennom more comfortable to play in, and dealing with the endgame.


